  <h2><span class="k">03</span>L0 — the schema everything waits on</h2>
  <p><strong>A proposal</strong>, synthesised primarily from Flue's accepted-work ledger and canonical
    stream plus eve's request-bound human-input protocol, with tenancy fields of our own. The surveyed
    frameworks do <em>not</em> share this full contract.</p>
<pre><code>submission   id · session · tenant/workspace · agent_type · payload
             admitted_at              -- written BEFORE any model work
             status                   -- queued | running | settled
             outcome                  -- completed | failed | aborted   (exactly one)
             idempotency_key?         -- our own; not a public Flue field

record       session · kind · payload · recorded_at
             batch_offset             -- opaque, assigned per ATOMIC APPEND BATCH;
                                      -- several records may share one offset
             instance_uid             -- immutable identity of this instance lifetime
             attempt_id · owner_epoch -- execution ownership and write fencing

pause        request_id · session · kind (approval | question)
             action + canonical_args  -- exactly what is being consented to
             state · answered_at · answer
             -- a stale answer never authorizes the original call</code></pre>

  <div class="box">
    <p><strong>Three rules.</strong> Admit durably before any model work. Exactly one terminal outcome
      per submission. <em>At-least-once execution over exactly-once recording.</em></p>
    <p style="margin-top:10px">On recovery, unresolved <strong>ordinary</strong> tool calls become
      explicit unknown outcomes and are not blindly retried. Durable tools and delegated tasks resume
      under their own replay rules — and <strong>no external effect is exactly-once merely because the
      record is</strong>. Idempotency stays the caller's job.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">Clients resume strictly <em>after</em> a returned offset and never derive offsets
    arithmetically. Physical storage topology — one database or many — stays a deployment choice; what
    is fixed is one authoritative stream per agent instance.</p>

  <h2><span class="k">04</span>What we keep</h2>
  <p>The audit's do-not-delete list is long and it is all one thing: <strong>governance</strong>.
    Tenant membership checks, MCP grants, lease fences, workspace path authority, submitter identity
    carried into tool calls, hosted durable-root selection — and scope verification on public gateway
    and live-connection operations.</p>
  <div class="box plain">
    <p>Of eleven frameworks surveyed, two ship a real authorization model. <strong>Mastra's production
      FGA is enterprise-licensed; LangGraph Platform supports custom per-resource auth across LangSmith
      plans</strong> while charging separately for deployment, compute, team and self-hosting.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:10px">So the sharper claim — <em>"tenancy is what the field charges for"</em> —
      does not follow from these two examples and has been withdrawn. What holds: <strong>almost no
      framework ships tenancy at all</strong>, and ours beats both of those that do on structural
      confinement while losing on entitlement discovery and central revocation. That gap is L7.</p>
  </div>

  <h2><span class="k">05</span>The honest minimum</h2>
  <p>None of L0–L7 ships a vertical agent. They are well-specified platform work.</p>
  <div class="box">
    <p><strong>Do L0 + L3. Stop there.</strong> Decide the record shape, land human intention on its
      pause record — the lane with a branch already open — and hold the rest until a paying vertical
      asks for a specific piece.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">Caveat from review: #1127 channel answering has no implementation, is deprioritised,
    and depends on more than L0. L3 delivers the workspace-side durable pause; the channel surface is a
    separate commitment.</p>

  <h2><span class="k">06</span>Genuinely open</h2>
  <p>Three of the five questions in rev 1 were already answered by the code. What remains:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>First-party agents only, or third-party authored?</strong> Decides whether
      isolation-per-agent ever matters, and therefore whether celld is ever relevant.</li>
    <li><strong>Do any flag-enabled deployments hold records recoverable only from the current event
      store?</strong> Blocks the store migration. Answerable by inspection.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Answered by main, and struck: <code>message-end.final</code> is one-message scoped;
    delayed tool results after final are explicitly reducer-supported and tested; the pinned pi version
    has no public selective follow-up cancellation, which is precisely why the private compatibility
    shim exists.</p>

  <h2><span class="k">07</span>What rev 1 got wrong</h2>
  <div class="tw">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Correction</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>"~3,700 lines"</td><td>Components total <strong>3,035</strong>. The larger figure only worked by silently counting all 1,315 lines of <code>harnessPiChatService</code>, contradicting the same table's ~600.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"three owners"</td><td>Four under the shipped flag-enabled configuration.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>L4 "pure deletion"</td><td>Mostly false. No custom compactor; skill parsing already delegates; tool schemas carry authority; truncation is a sandbox contract; diff is a renderer.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"eleven frameworks agree" on the schema</td><td>Fabricated. It is a proposal synthesised from two sources, relabelled accordingly.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>incarnation</code> = "which process generation served it"</td><td>Wrong semantics — Flue's incarnation is instance lifetime identity. Execution ownership is <code>attempt_id</code>/<code>owner_epoch</code>. The original wording would have encoded a bad fencing invariant.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"opaque cursor" per record</td><td>Offsets are assigned per <strong>atomic append batch</strong>; several records can share one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"never silently retried" (unqualified)</td><td>True for ordinary tools only. Durable tools and delegated tasks replay.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"OTel redacted by default"</td><td><strong>Reversed.</strong> Flue's trace adapters capture conversation content <em>by default</em> — prompts, outputs, tool arguments and results. Content-free capture requires opting out. A data-governance error, not a wording one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"both paywall tenancy"</td><td>False for LangGraph. Thesis withdrawn.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>#979 = "implicit sessions"</td><td>Native addressed first-send. #1009's replay durability is already wired and is not the proposed settlement system.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>L6/L7 "independent" vs "everything blocks something else"</td><td>Self-contradictory. They are deferred on priority, not blocked.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

