   122	  <div class="box fix">
   123	    <p><strong>Corrected:</strong> L4 was previously described as broad "pure deletion" of pi
   124	      duplication. Review of main found that overstated — there is <em>no</em> custom LLM compactor,
   125	      skill parsing already delegates to pi, tool schemas and adaptation carry host authority,
   126	      truncation is a sandbox contract, and the diff is a React renderer. Only the raw transcript
   127	      wrappers are true duplication.</p>
   128	  </div>
   129	
   130	  <h2><span class="k">03</span>L0 — the schema everything waits on</h2>
   131	  <p><strong>A proposal</strong>, synthesised primarily from Flue's accepted-work ledger and canonical
   132	    stream plus eve's request-bound human-input protocol, with tenancy fields of our own. The surveyed
   133	    frameworks do <em>not</em> share this full contract.</p>
   134	<pre><code>submission   id · session · tenant/workspace · agent_type · payload
   135	             admitted_at              -- written BEFORE any model work
   136	             status                   -- queued | running | settled
   137	             outcome                  -- completed | failed | aborted   (exactly one)
   138	             idempotency_key?         -- our own; not a public Flue field
   139	
   140	record       session · kind · payload · recorded_at
   141	             batch_offset             -- opaque, assigned per ATOMIC APPEND BATCH;
   142	                                      -- several records may share one offset
   143	             instance_uid             -- immutable identity of this instance lifetime
   144	             attempt_id · owner_epoch -- execution ownership and write fencing
   145	
   146	pause        request_id · session · kind (approval | question)
   147	             action + canonical_args  -- exactly what is being consented to
   148	             state · answered_at · answer
   149	             -- a stale answer never authorizes the original call</code></pre>
   150	
   151	  <div class="box">
   152	    <p><strong>Three rules.</strong> Admit durably before any model work. Exactly one terminal outcome
   153	      per submission. <em>At-least-once execution over exactly-once recording.</em></p>
   154	    <p style="margin-top:10px">On recovery, unresolved <strong>ordinary</strong> tool calls become
   155	      explicit unknown outcomes and are not blindly retried. Durable tools and delegated tasks resume
   156	      under their own replay rules — and <strong>no external effect is exactly-once merely because the
   157	      record is</strong>. Idempotency stays the caller's job.</p>
   158	  </div>
   159	  <p class="muted">Clients resume strictly <em>after</em> a returned offset and never derive offsets
   160	    arithmetically. Physical storage topology — one database or many — stays a deployment choice; what
   161	    is fixed is one authoritative stream per agent instance.</p>
   162	
   163	  <h2><span class="k">04</span>What we keep</h2>
   164	  <p>The audit's do-not-delete list is long and it is all one thing: <strong>governance</strong>.
   165	    Tenant membership checks, MCP grants, lease fences, workspace path authority, submitter identity
   166	    carried into tool calls, hosted durable-root selection — and scope verification on public gateway
   167	    and live-connection operations.</p>
   168	  <div class="box plain">
   169	    <p>Of eleven frameworks surveyed, two ship a real authorization model. <strong>Mastra's production
   170	      FGA is enterprise-licensed; LangGraph Platform supports custom per-resource auth across LangSmith
   171	      plans</strong> while charging separately for deployment, compute, team and self-hosting.</p>
   172	    <p style="margin-top:10px">So the sharper claim — <em>"tenancy is what the field charges for"</em> —
   173	      does not follow from these two examples and has been withdrawn. What holds: <strong>almost no
   174	      framework ships tenancy at all</strong>, and ours beats both of those that do on structural
   175	      confinement while losing on entitlement discovery and central revocation. That gap is L7.</p>
   176	  </div>
   177	
   178	  <h2><span class="k">05</span>The honest minimum</h2>
   179	  <p>None of L0–L7 ships a vertical agent. They are well-specified platform work.</p>
   180	  <div class="box">
   181	    <p><strong>Do L0 + L3. Stop there.</strong> Decide the record shape, land human intention on its
   182	      pause record — the lane with a branch already open — and hold the rest until a paying vertical
   183	      asks for a specific piece.</p>
   184	  </div>
   185	  <p class="muted">Caveat from review: #1127 channel answering has no implementation, is deprioritised,
   186	    and depends on more than L0. L3 delivers the workspace-side durable pause; the channel surface is a
   187	    separate commitment.</p>
   188	
   189	  <h2><span class="k">06</span>Genuinely open</h2>
   190	  <p>Three of the five questions in rev 1 were already answered by the code. What remains:</p>
   191	  <ul>
   192	    <li><strong>First-party agents only, or third-party authored?</strong> Decides whether
   193	      isolation-per-agent ever matters, and therefore whether celld is ever relevant.</li>
   194	    <li><strong>Do any flag-enabled deployments hold records recoverable only from the current event
   195	      store?</strong> Blocks the store migration. Answerable by inspection.</li>
   196	  </ul>
   197	  <p class="muted">Answered by main, and struck: <code>message-end.final</code> is one-message scoped;
   198	    delayed tool results after final are explicitly reducer-supported and tested; the pinned pi version
   199	    has no public selective follow-up cancellation, which is precisely why the private compatibility
   200	    shim exists.</p>
